      "cards": [
        {
          "id": "c2_1_7149",
          "topic": "Algorithm Definition and Properties",
          "question": "What constitutes an algorithm in the context of computer science, and what are its essential properties?",
          "answer": "• An algorithm is a finite, unambiguous sequence of precise instructions designed to perform a specific task or solve a well-defined problem.\n• Key properties include finiteness (terminates after a finite number of steps), definiteness (each step is precisely defined), input (zero or more external quantities), output (at least one produced quantity), and effectiveness (each operation must be sufficiently basic to be executable by a human or machine)."
        },
        {
          "id": "c2_2_5dd9",
          "topic": "Foundational Disciplines of Computer Science",
          "question": "Identify the primary academic disciplines that form the foundational bedrock of Computer Science.",
          "answer": "• Mathematics: Provides the formalisms for logic, discrete structures, graph theory, calculus, and probability, which are essential for algorithmic analysis, data structures, complexity theory, and theoretical computing.\n• Engineering: Contributes principles of system design, hardware architecture, circuit theory, control systems, and practical implementation methodologies for constructing robust and efficient computational systems."
        },
